{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfBT4giiDu8ZGxhEZWUAs","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I submitted index 22 (In 0.25 / Sb 0.75), which had the highest EQE I measured at 0.0176%. I only measured 3 of the 28 compositions, so I can't confirm it's the best in the library. That depends on the unmeasured points.\n\nThe three measured EQEs were:\n\n| Index | In / Sb | EQE (%) | What XRD showed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 5 | 0.80 / 0.20 | 0.0057 | Two phases: rutile InSbO4 plus In₂O₃ |\n| 15 | 0.49 / 0.51 | 0.0174 | Almost pure InSbO4 |\n| 22 | 0.25 / 0.75 | 0.0176 | Not measured |\n\n- **XRD:** I used 3 of 5 scans, at indices 5, 15 and 24. Rutile InSbO4 shows up across the whole line. The In-rich end also has In₂O₃ (bixbyite), and index 24 has a couple of small extra peaks that I couldn't identify.\n- **Merit:** I spent all 3 EQE measurements. The In-rich, two-phase sample was about 3× worse. Indices 15 and 22 are nearly tied, so the Sb-rich side is flat or only slightly better; it isn't a clear peak. A composition between those two, or further toward Sb, could still beat index 22.\n\nAll EQE values are very low. That fits a wide-gap oxide that absorbs little light at 2.7 eV."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":407,"cache_read_input_tokens":8554,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":407},"output_tokens":498,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bea2e0b5-dd37-4aa6-aea6-adfeb6a8c1c5","uuid":"779554ca-bf67-4f28-b7ac-be159da0c975","timestamp":"2026-09-18T19:23:15.036Z","request_id":"req_011CfBT4gEBxQQ5HGkq7fyAM"}
